{"type":"text","timestamp":1794319258431,"sessionID":"ses_e3a41e784ffewW0a3KF7BSFPAW","part":{"id":"prt_1c5c3132f001JBXIIdiRd46jZT","messageID":"msg_1c5c305fc001wRCQ0XOJP59tGC","sessionID":"ses_e3a41e784ffewW0a3KF7BSFPAW","type":"text","text":"<think>Interesting — the proxy index may be a local mirror that has limited packages, or pip is confused. The message \"The user requested (constraint) flask==3.1.3\" conflicting with itself is weird. This suggests the index doesn't have flask 3.1.3, or there's a hash mismatch causing it to be treated differently.\n\nWait, actually: the error is typically seen when the pinned version is not available... no, that gives \"No matching distribution\". Hmm, \"The user requested flask==3.1.3 / The user requested (constraint) flask==3.1.3\" — this happens when pip's resolver can't find any candidate for flask that satisfies both, likely because the available flask versions are empty (proxy index empty for that package?). Earlier `pip download pyjwt` said \"from versions: none\" — the index seems mostly empty. So the mirror only has some packages? Let me check what the index offers for flask.\n\nMaybe the sandbox has a local cache directory with wheels. Let me look for pip cache or a local wheelhouse.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794319258415,"end":1794319258428}}}
